  <h2>Gaming Nickname Ideas by Role</h2>

  <h3>Competitive / Ranked Players</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rank display:</strong> "Jett²⁰²⁶" — show your peak rank year without looking boastful</li>
    <li><strong>Role tags:</strong> "ᵐᵃⁿⁿᵈᴳᵃᵐᵉʳ" for support mains in MOBA games</li>
    <li><strong>Clan tags:</strong> "[ᴀᴄᴇ]PlayerName" — small caps clan tag looks like an official badge</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Casual / Social Players</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fun names:</strong> "ⓉⓇⓎⒽⒶⓇⒹ" (TRYHARD in bubble text) — self-aware humor</li>
    <li><strong>Status names:</strong> "ᵃᶠᵏ_Farmer" for Minecraft or open-world games</li>
    <li><strong>Theme names:</strong> "ᴛʜᴇᴅʀᴀɢᴏɴ" in small caps has fantasy RPG vibes</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Streamers &amp; Content Creators</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Brand names:</strong> "𝐒𝐭𝐫𝐞𝐚𝐦𝐞𝐫𝐍𝐚𝐦𝐞" in bold stands out in Twitch chat and on overlays</li>
    <li><strong>Alt accounts:</strong> "MainAlt⁰²" — superscript numbers distinguish alts without cluttering the name</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Important Warnings for Competitive Play</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Tournament rules vary:</strong> Many esports tournaments require standard ASCII names for broadcast readability. A name that's fine in ranked play may be rejected in a tournament. Always check the rulebook</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting risk:</strong> Names that are hard to read, memorize, or type can lead to false reports. Keep your name identifiable — don't make the entire thing Wingdings-style gibberish</li>
    <li><strong>Patch changes:</strong> Game developers update character filters without warning. A name that works today may break after the next patch. Superscript numbers (⁰¹²³⁴⁵⁶⁷⁸⁹) are the most stable — they've been in Unicode since version 1.0</li>
    <li><strong>Console limitations:</strong> PlayStation, Xbox, and Nintendo Switch have stricter character filters than PC. If you play cross-platform, test your name on console before committing to a paid name change</li>
  </ol>



  <h3>Overwatch 2</h3>
  <p><strong>Compatibility: Good.</strong> Blizzard's hero shooter accepts superscript and Small Caps in BattleTag names. The 12-character BattleTag limit makes Small Caps particularly useful — a name like "á´˜Ê€á´á´…ÉªÉ¢Ê" (PRODIGY in small caps) uses the same character count as "PRODIGY" but looks more distinctive. Superscript numbers are also reliable for adding a year tag ("á´˜â±¼áµƒÊ³â¿ xâ·€"). Bubble Text is blocked by Blizzard's filter.</p>
  <h3>Call of Duty (Warzone, Modern Warfare)</h3>
  <p><strong>Compatibility: Limited.</strong> Activision's name filter is among the most restrictive in gaming. Username changes require a rename token, and the filter blocks most Unicode characters beyond basic Latin. Superscript numbers (¹²³) work on Xbox and PlayStation COD profiles but are rejected on PC. Small Caps, Bold, and Bubble Text are universally blocked.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>What works:</strong> On console COD profiles only — superscript numbers appended to a standard name</li>
    <li><strong>What doesn't:</strong> PC Battle.net profiles, full small text names, any styled letters</li>
    <li><strong>Workaround:</strong> Use clan tags for Unicode flair instead. Clan tags have fewer restrictions</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Crossplay &amp; Console Considerations</h2>
  <p>If you play cross-platform (PC + console), your small text name must render on both. This is the most common failure point for gaming nicknames. <strong>PlayStation and Xbox</strong> use system-level character filters that are often stricter than individual game filters. A name that works on Valorant PC may show as blank boxes on Valorant console.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>PlayStation Network:</strong> Restrictive. Only superscript numbers reliably pass. Small Caps and Bold are blocked</li>
    <li><strong>Xbox Live:</strong> Similar restrictions to PSN. Use superscript numbers only for cross-platform play</li>
    <li><strong>Nintendo Switch:</strong> Most restrictive. Virtually no Unicode styling survives Nintendo's name filter</li>
    <li><strong>Steam Deck:</strong> Same as Steam PC — full Unicode support</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Golden rule for crossplay:</strong> If your friend group spans platforms, use superscript numbers only. They're the lowest common denominator across all platform filters.</p>

